DOUGLASS = ("<div class='card' style='border-left-color:#C9A84C'><h3 style='margin-top:0'>From \"What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?\"</h3><p class='muted' style='margin-top:-6px'>Frederick Douglass, speech delivered in Rochester, New York, July 5, 1852 (text as printed in the appendix of his book My Bondage and My Freedom, 1855). Paragraphs are numbered for discussion; ... marks words left out.</p>"
            "<p style='font-family:Georgia,serif'><b>[1]</b> Must I undertake to prove that the slave is a man? That point is conceded already. Nobody doubts it. The slaveholders themselves acknowledge it in the enactment of laws for their government. ... The manhood of the slave is conceded. It is admitted in the fact that southern statute books are covered with enactments forbidding, under severe fines and penalties, the teaching of the slave to read or write.</p>"
            "<p style='font-family:Georgia,serif'><b>[2]</b> Would you have me argue that man is entitled to liberty? that he is the rightful owner of his own body? You have already declared it.</p>"
            "<p style='font-family:Georgia,serif'><b>[3]</b> At a time like this, scorching irony, not convincing argument, is needed. ... For it is not light that is needed, but fire; it is not the gentle shower, but thunder.</p>"
            "<p style='font-family:Georgia,serif'><b>[4]</b> What to the American slave is your Fourth of July? I answer, a day that reveals to him, more than all other days in the year, the gross injustice and cruelty to which he is the constant victim.</p>"
            "<p class='muted' style='font-size:12px'>conceded: admitted to be true. enactment: the making of a law. statute books: books of written laws. entitled: having a right to. irony: saying the opposite of what you mean, or pointing out a painful contradiction, to make a point.</p></div>")

